  "source": "library/titles.html (certified 2026-07-17) — Son of Man / Daniel 7 / the trial / Kyrios, plus the essay's own cumulative-pattern bound",
  "voiceover": "Jesus' favourite name for himself was Son of Man. He used it about eighty times in the Gospels, almost always about himself. And to our ears it sounds humble. Son of man. A human being. That is how God addresses the prophet Ezekiel, and that ordinary sense is real. But the background is Daniel chapter seven. There the prophet sees one like a son of man, coming with the clouds of heaven. He approaches the Ancient of Days, and he receives dominion, and glory, and the service of all nations — a kingdom that never ends. And coming with the clouds is imagery the Hebrew Bible uses of God's own appearing. So at his trial, asked directly who he is, Jesus fuses Daniel seven with Psalm one hundred and ten, and claims to be the heavenly figure enthroned beside God. Then there is Lord. Kyrios. When the Greek Old Testament was read aloud in the synagogues, the divine name was spoken as Kyrios. And Paul writes: confess that Jesus is Lord, and you will be saved — for everyone who calls on the name of the Lord will be saved. He is quoting a line about calling on YHWH. No single title settles it, and we should say so. Messiah and Son of David are royal, and human. But read together, they place Jesus inside what Israel reserved for her God alone — and that is a remarkable place for the evidence to begin.",
